{
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/master/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "CRSBench-HintGenerator",
          "version": "1.0.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/your-org/crsbench",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-193",
              "name": "CWE-193: Off-by-one Error",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Off-by-one Error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Specific vulnerability type: Off-by-one Error"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-122",
              "name": "CWE-122: Heap-based Buffer Overflow",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Heap-based Buffer Overflow"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Specific vulnerability type: Heap-based Buffer Overflow"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-787",
              "name": "CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Out-of-bounds Write"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Specific vulnerability type: Out-of-bounds Write"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-193",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "CWE-193 - Off-by-one Error\nVulnerability: Off-by-One Heap Buffer Overflow in buffer_write Function\nDescription: A heap-based buffer overflow vulnerability exists in the buffer_write function \ndue to an off-by-one error in the bounds check. The function uses an incorrect \ncomparison operator (>) instead of (>=) when validating the index parameter, \nallowing an attacker to write one byte past the end of a heap-allocated buffer.\n\nFor a buffer of size 300, valid indices range from 0 to 299. However, the \nvulnerable check 'if (index > buf->size)' only rejects indices greater than 300, \nallowing index 300 to pass through. This results in writing to buf->buf[300], \nwhich is one byte beyond the allocated region at address offset 0x5120000005ec.\n\nThe vulnerability can corrupt adjacent heap metadata or other heap objects, \npotentially leading to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or \ninformation disclosure depending on heap layout.\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "utils.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 21,
                  "endLine": 24,
                  "startColumn": 9,
                  "endColumn": 27
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "buffer_write",
                  "kind": "function"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-122",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "CWE-122 - Heap-based Buffer Overflow\nVulnerability: Off-by-One Heap Buffer Overflow in buffer_write Function\nDescription: A heap-based buffer overflow vulnerability exists in the buffer_write function \ndue to an off-by-one error in the bounds check. The function uses an incorrect \ncomparison operator (>) instead of (>=) when validating the index parameter, \nallowing an attacker to write one byte past the end of a heap-allocated buffer.\n\nFor a buffer of size 300, valid indices range from 0 to 299. However, the \nvulnerable check 'if (index > buf->size)' only rejects indices greater than 300, \nallowing index 300 to pass through. This results in writing to buf->buf[300], \nwhich is one byte beyond the allocated region at address offset 0x5120000005ec.\n\nThe vulnerability can corrupt adjacent heap metadata or other heap objects, \npotentially leading to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or \ninformation disclosure depending on heap layout.\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "utils.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 21,
                  "endLine": 24,
                  "startColumn": 9,
                  "endColumn": 27
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "buffer_write",
                  "kind": "function"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-787",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "CWE-787 - Out-of-bounds Write\nVulnerability: Off-by-One Heap Buffer Overflow in buffer_write Function\nDescription: A heap-based buffer overflow vulnerability exists in the buffer_write function \ndue to an off-by-one error in the bounds check. The function uses an incorrect \ncomparison operator (>) instead of (>=) when validating the index parameter, \nallowing an attacker to write one byte past the end of a heap-allocated buffer.\n\nFor a buffer of size 300, valid indices range from 0 to 299. However, the \nvulnerable check 'if (index > buf->size)' only rejects indices greater than 300, \nallowing index 300 to pass through. This results in writing to buf->buf[300], \nwhich is one byte beyond the allocated region at address offset 0x5120000005ec.\n\nThe vulnerability can corrupt adjacent heap metadata or other heap objects, \npotentially leading to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or \ninformation disclosure depending on heap layout.\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "utils.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 21,
                  "endLine": 24,
